r some reason  the fifth  xte GPU is scoring very low compared to  the 5600 M which scored about 43,000  points we realized that there was some  issues with the drivers so we had to  hook up a second display to the a GPU to  get the full power now quite a bit  higher than the 5600 M and by the way  protip if you don't want to use two  displays you can use the link below to  buy an HDMI dummy adapter which tricks  the system into thinking it's a display  thus giving you the better performance  but even with that the 5700 XT is still  not scoring as high as it would if it  wasn't a desktop PC and that's because  of the fact that it's an eg PU and it's  sending all of that data through a  single Thunderbolt 3 cable so the lower  performance is understandable now moving  on to the unit in heaven gaming test the  5600 M MacBook Pro by itself  surprisingly outperformed the e GPU  setups even though it scored lower in  Geekbench 5 which is probably because  heaven uses OpenGL instead of metal we  then tested GFX bench 5.0 which actually  uses metal and in the Aztec ruins test  the e GPU system was around 40% faster  than the 5600 M MacBook Pro and in the  Manhattan test there was an even bigger  difference so it's crazy to see this  performance jump compared to the heaven  test now before we get into comparing  actual gaming performance let's do some  Final Cut Pro 10 video editing tests and  most of these are heavily graphics  dependent so the difference is in CPUs  won't really affect the results as much  as other tests do starting with the  Bruce X benchmark which is almost purely  graphics dependent we can see that the  5600 M is performing incredibly well and  the EGP you setups are taking quite a  bit longer which is interesting because  we've seen better results in the past  but maybe the e GPU performance slowed  down with the new versions of Mac OS or  Final Cut Pro next up in our one minute  HEV C stabilization test the 5600 M  destroyed the other two once again 